($q$B$q$,$q$03d4a598-d027-449a-896a-205d982f12c5$q$::uuid,$q$A1$q$,$q$So for Quiz A, z = (86-74)/8 = 12/8 = 1.5. For Quiz B, z = (62-52)/4 = 10/4 = 2.5. Since 2.5 is higher, they did relatively better on Quiz B. Z-scores are useful because they put different scores on the same scale, showing how many standard deviations above or below the mean each score is.$q$,$q$deepseek$q$,$q${"sig":"1111","type":"A1"}$q$::jsonb,$q$1b5676839dc5e00ea69487587e2ecdd1b8fa7cd7ace5e3b3a26f3eb4485b4c9a$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$accf82300d7abca7bed0f7bbc6a389da97190fada7caa7efe78f2c66626c0b5b$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$03d4a598-d027-449a-896a-205d982f12c5$q$::uuid,$q$A2$q$,$q$For Quiz A: (86-74)/8 = 12/8 which gives me 1.5

For Quiz B I get (62-52)/4 = 10/4 = 2.5

