($q$B$q$,$q$03d4a598-d027-449a-896a-205d982f12c5$q$::uuid,$q$A7$q$,$q$So for Quiz A, z = (86 - 74)/8 = 12/8 = 1.5. For Quiz B, I think it’s (62 - 52)/4 = 10/4 = 2.25, so that’s higher relative to the mean. So she did better on Quiz B compared to her class, since z-scores just tell you how many standard deviations above or below the mean you are, which makes different tests comparable.$q$,$q$deepseek$q$,$q${"sig":"1011","type":"A7"}$q$::jsonb,$q$f30f9dbdfd0cc084f97ec559f9bda66eed09e41d46df67965784916f7a70c276$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$accf82300d7abca7bed0f7bbc6a389da97190fada7caa7efe78f2c66626c0b5b$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$b0bd8a7f-333e-45c8-8cf4-07a13fb6b4f4$q$::uuid,$q$A1$q$,$q$For each additional hour a student studies, their predicted test score increases by about 4.1 points on average. The association between study hours and test scores is strong and positive. A student studying for 6 hours would be predicted to score 76.6. If they actually scored 74, the residual is -2.6, meaning their observed score was 2.6 points lower than predicted.$q$,$q$google$q$,$q${"sig":"1111","type":"A1"}$q$::jsonb,$q$52fa7ee0f34e9d1de9e528ecee14a769643d8caa0f161004118fbae9d83aedd9$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$c832b32bf29ca2bb5626ae588ca0d0f649b27e0fd3817d0dd912410c33746039$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$b0bd8a7f-333e-45c8-8cf4-07a13fb6b4f4$q$::uuid,$q$A2$q$,$q$So if they add an hour of studying, the score tends to go up by roughly 4.1 points—like, on average. The scatterplot looked pretty tight and going up, so I’d say it’s a strong positive thing. Plugging in 6 gives 52 + 24.6 = 76.6, but the kid actually got a 74, so the residual is 74 - 76.6 = -2.6, meaning they did worse than the line expected by about two and a half points.$q$,$q$deepseek$q$,$q${"sig":"1111","type":"A2"}$q$::jsonb,$q$e47113fee0a85645e2e597affd256b1ff6fe77b80ee0151959f728d71a44e5a8$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$c832b32bf29ca2bb5626ae588ca0d0f649b27e0fd3817d0dd912410c33746039$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$b0bd8a7f-333e-45c8-8cf4-07a13fb6b4f4$q$::uuid,$q$A3$q$,$q$The slope means that for each additional hour a student studies, their test score goes up by about 4.1 points on average. The relationship is strong and positive since r = 0.93 which is close to 1. For a student who studied 6 hours, the predicted score would be 52 + 4.1(6) = 52 + 24.6 = 76.6.$q$,$q$anthropic$q$,$q${"sig":"1110","type":"A3"}$q$::jsonb,$q$c2e263f5e5d6d1db687f9f5e4b644853baaafe65f0ce404ec217e94222e71dc9$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$c832b32bf29ca2bb5626ae588ca0d0f649b27e0fd3817d0dd912410c33746039$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$b0bd8a7f-333e-45c8-8cf4-07a13fb6b4f4$q$::uuid,$q$A4$q$,$q$The association between study hours and test scores is strong and positive. For a student studying 6 hours, the predicted score would be 52 plus 4.1 times 6. If their actual score was 74, the residual is -2.6, meaning their score was 2.6 points lower than predicted.$q$,$q$google$q$,$q${"sig":"0101","type":"A4"}$q$::jsonb,$q$393c492eb4c0e404d85f83acb237d97c2614d30b23a00662be6bf95d24b12abf$q$,$q$reader_queue$q$,$q$c832b32bf29ca2bb5626ae588ca0d0f649b27e0fd3817d0dd912410c33746039$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$b0bd8a7f-333e-45c8-8cf4-07a13fb6b4f4$q$::uuid,$q$A5$q$,$q$The slope means for every extra hour of studying, the predicted score goes up by about 4.1 points, so more studying is linked to higher scores. For 6 hours, the line gives 52 + 4.1(6) = 76.6, but the actual score was 74, so the residual is 74 - 76.6 = -2.6, meaning they scored lower than expected. The correlation is pretty high, so the association is pretty tight.$q$,$q$deepseek$q$,$q${"sig":"1011","type":"A5"}$q$::jsonb,$q$4072bdbfcbd375c3322a87965b9802af2da26b9fa02ec325d7b1529760b8a830$q$,$q$reader_queue$q$,$q$c832b32bf29ca2bb5626ae588ca0d0f649b27e0fd3817d0dd912410c33746039$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$b0bd8a7f-333e-45c8-8cf4-07a13fb6b4f4$q$::uuid,$q$A6$q$,$q$The slope means that for each extra hour of studying, the test score goes up by around 4.1 points. The correlation of 0.93 shows a strong positive relationship between study time and test scores. For 6 hours, the predicted score is 52 + 4.1(6) = 76.6. The residual would be 74 - 76.6 which is negative, so the actual score was a bit lower than what we'd expect from the regression line.$q$,$q$anthropic$q$,$q${"sig":"1110","type":"A6"}$q$::jsonb,$q$cd02ea5c54033736593a4f110941f46237fcecd49442c7d806bbdffdddc4d274$q$,$q$reader_queue$q$,$q$c832b32bf29ca2bb5626ae588ca0d0f649b27e0fd3817d0dd912410c33746039$q$);